{
  "teamId": 142,
  "year": 2025,
  "narrative": "The 2025 Minnesota Twins were a team that never quite found their footing, finishing with a 70-92 record and well out of playoff contention. From the opening weeks of the season, Minnesota was plagued by a pitching staff that surrendered runs at an alarming rate — a 4.55 ERA that reflected a bullpen constantly letting games slip away at the worst possible moments. Early April and May offered some hope in the form of clutch hits from Brooks Lee and DaShawn Keirsey Jr., but for every bright moment, a devastating homer allowed by Cole Sands, Louis Varland, or Simeon Woods Richardson seemed to follow.\n\nThe summer months told the story of a team with individual bright spots buried within systemic failure. Harrison Bader emerged as an unlikely hero, launching back-to-back-to-back high-leverage home runs in a remarkable July 4th weekend stretch that briefly energized the fanbase. Brooks Lee's line-drive single on July 28th, clearing the bases and scoring three, was the kind of play that made fans believe — for a moment — that this team could turn it around. But those moments of hope were fleeting, punctuated by the season's single most damaging play: Griffin Jax's inability to retire Freddie Freeman on July 23rd, a single that scored Mookie Betts, Shohei Ohtani, and more in a crushing late-game collapse against the Dodgers.\n\nAugust brought the season's most dramatic swings. Royce Lewis delivered a grand slam and Matt Wallner hit his 20th home run in a three-run shot that briefly made the box scores look respectable. Rookie Luke Keaschall announced himself with a clutch two-run blast. But the bullpen — particularly Cole Sands, Michael Tonkin, and Justin Topa — could not hold leads when it mattered. A devastating Justin Topa double-allowed on September 3rd, surrendering two runs on a sharp Michael A. Taylor line drive, felt like the moment the season truly died.\n\nSeptember was a formality, a grim march through meaningless games. Cole Sands allowed a three-run homer to Gabriel Moreno that carried a WPA swing of -0.648, emblematic of a bullpen that burned down the house all year. The one bright note in the season's final weeks was Kody Clemens, who singled home three runners on September 13th in a late-season win that meant nothing in the standings but everything to the players who showed up every day. The 2025 Twins scored 678 runs but allowed 773, a damning run differential that told the whole story — a team with enough offense to stay interesting but a pitching staff that ensured they never truly were.",
